userStories.description:
**Goal** — Add an orthogonal stale axis to every manifest excluded-chunk entry without changing its mechanical exclusion reason.

**Motivation** — Staleness demotes a chunk's score — it never excludes it, and the exclusion site records only the mechanical cause, so the attribution is lost.

**Approach** — Staleness is an axis, not a reason. The mechanical cause of an exclusion (`budget`, `below-min-score`, `dedupe`, `role-filter`) and the fact that the chunk was stale are orthogonal facts, and the current `reason` union conflates them by offering `"stale"` as a fifth alternative. Adding a separate `stale` flag preserves both facts; overwriting `reason` would destroy the mechanical cause. The `"stale"` member of the union is therefore removed — nothing produces it and nothing will.

The flag is stamped **uniformly on all five exclusion-stamping sites** — the four mappings inside `buildManifest` plus the inline construction in `rebuild.ts` — so the contract does not depend on which paths production happens to reach today. Production reachability is narrower than that contract: per the Motivation, only `dedupe` and `role-filter` can carry a stale chunk. The unit criteria below pin the uniform stamping; the single integration criterion drives the one path a real stale chunk can reach.

**Scope** — In: manifest schema, `ManifestInputs.staleIds`, all exclusion mappings, orchestrator stale-ID derivation, and rebuild budget-exclusion attribution. Out: forwarding observation fields and retargeting H5 belongs to US-002; provenance belongs to US-003; retention belongs to US-004; making stale chunks reachable by budget/min-score exclusion remains deferred.

**Interface**
```typescript
ContextManifest["excludedChunks"]: Array<{
  id: string;
  reason: "below-min-score" | "budget" | "dedupe" | "role-filter";
  stale?: boolean;
}>

interface ManifestInputs {
  staleIds: ReadonlySet<string>;
}
```

`buildManifest` has exactly one production caller. The set is derived there from `scored`, which is in scope and is documented as a superset of every chunk that reaches the exclusion lists. `rebuild.ts` does **not** call `buildManifest`; its budget mapping additionally stamps `stale`, derived from the `packedChunks` in scope, which carry `staleCandidate`.

**Goal** — Forward the persisted provider and excluded-chunk stale fields into curator observations and make H5 select the stale axis.

**Motivation** — In the measured `acp-catalog-pricing` run, 1,202 of 1,294 observations (93%) were `chunk-included` and fed no heuristic, while `chunkProviders` is already persisted onto the manifest and the emitter drops it.

**Approach** — The provider is the grouping axis. `chunkProviders` is already persisted, so carrying `provider` onto both chunk observations is plumbing, not new state. `ChunkKind` is deliberately **not** persisted: it is load-bearing in memory (`scoring.ts:31` `KIND_WEIGHTS`, and the floor logic in `packing.ts`) and for every built-in provider it is near-1:1 with the provider id (`git-history`→`history`, `lint-config`→`lint-config`, `test-coverage`→`test-coverage`, `static-rules`→`static`), so persisting it would buy a second axis that mostly duplicates the first.

**Scope** — In: curator observation contracts, manifest collector projection, and H5 filtering. Out: producing the manifest stale flag belongs to US-001; provenance belongs to US-003; retention belongs to US-004; persisting ChunkKind and adding a heuristic are excluded.

**Interface**
```typescript
ChunkIncludedObservation.payload: {
  chunkId: string;
  label: string;
  tokens: number;
  provider?: string;
}

ChunkExcludedObservation.payload: {
  chunkId: string;
  label: string;
  reason?: string;
  provider?: string;
  stale?: boolean;
}
```

`h5StaleChunk` selects `chunk-excluded` observations where `payload.stale === true`. Its `staleChunkRuns` threshold and cross-run grouping by `chunkId` are unchanged. A missing `chunkProviders` entry omits `provider` rather than setting a placeholder.

**Goal** — Add configurable, failure-tolerant post-run pruning that rewrites project rollup rows only after the rollup exceeds a size threshold.

**Motivation** — Appending unconditionally while pruning only on manual invocation is not a retention policy, and most of a measured 219 MB rollup was already beyond the maximum heuristic-window tail.

**Approach** — Pruning is size-gated. `pruneRollup` is a two-pass, project-scoped, full-file rewrite under a path lock, and `scanProjectRunIds` full-scans the file before it. Running that after every run is not affordable. Reading `Bun.file(path).size` is effectively free, so the post-run hook reads the size first and does nothing below the threshold. The threshold and keep-count are configuration, not literals, so they are tunable and testable.

**Scope** — In: curator retention schema/runtime types and defaults, required curator type extraction/re-export, injectable auto-prune size gate and untyped post-run resolver, post-run invocation and warning behavior. Out: US-003 owns its provenance edits to `curator/index.ts`; per-run artifact deletion remains manual-gc-only; concurrent automatic pruning and manual gc remain deferred.

**Interface**
```typescript
maybePruneRollup(input: {
  rollupPath: string;
  projectKey: string;
  retention: CuratorRetentionConfig;
}): Promise<{ pruned: boolean; result?: PruneResult; error?: string }>
```

`CuratorConfigSchema` and `CuratorConfig` additionally carry `retention: { pruneThresholdBytes: number; keepRuns: number }`, with zod defaults `67108864` (64 MiB) and `50`. `maybePruneRollup` reads the size via `Bun.file(input.rollupPath).size`, returns `{ pruned: false }` unchanged when the size is at or below `retention.pruneThresholdBytes`, and otherwise derives `keepRunIds` from the first `retention.keepRuns` ids of `scanProjectRunIds` before calling `pruneRollup`. A rejection from either call is caught and reported on `error` with `pruned: false`.

Add a `DEFAULT_RETENTION` constant and `getCuratorRetention(context)` resolver mirroring the existing threshold pair because `PostRunContext.config` is `unknown`. Move `CuratorThresholds` and `CuratorConfig` wholesale with `CuratorRetentionConfig` into `src/config/runtime-types-curator.ts`; replace their old definitions in the full `runtime-types.ts` with `export type { CuratorConfig, CuratorRetentionConfig, CuratorThresholds } from "./runtime-types-curator";` so every external importer keeps working. A bare re-export does not bind those names in local scope, so the in-file reference at runtime-types.ts:564 must also be rewritten as `curator?: import("./runtime-types-curator").CuratorConfig;` (the idiom its neighbours mcp?, project?, debate? and autoPr already use); leaving it unchanged is an unresolved-name error. Reading size and pruning failures log `logger.warn` and return success from post-run; the curator is an observer. Automatic pruning invokes the existing locked rollup rewrite and does not delete artifacts.
